 r'].values[0]\nage_tbut_p = table2[table2['Clinical Marker'] == 'AVG_TBUT']['Pearson p-value'].values[0]\nage_osdi_p = table2[table2['Clinical Marker'] == 'OSDI']['Pearson p-value'].values[0]\n\n# Get severity distribution\nsev_counts = df['DRY_EYE_STATUS'].value_counts()\ntotal_patients = len(df)\nfemale_pct = (df['GENDER'] == 2).mean() * 100\n\nreport = f\"\"\"\n================================================================================\n          AGE-WISE PREDICTION OF DRY EYE DISEASE: \n          A MACHINE LEARNING APPROACH USING CLINICAL PARAMETERS\n================================================================================\n                              Research Report\n                              Date: {today}\n\n================================================================================\n1. ABSTRACT\n================================================================================\n\nBackground: Dry Eye Disease (DED) is a multifactorial ocular surface disorder \naffecting millions worldwide, with prevalence increasing significantly with age. \nEarly identification of age-related risk is critical for timely intervention.\n\nObjective: To develop and validate machine learning models for age-wise \nprediction of severe dry eye disease using standard clinical parameters \nincluding Tear Break-Up Time (TBUT), Tear Meniscus Height (TMH), Ocular \nSurface Disease Index (OSDI), and Blink Rate.\n\nMethods: A cross-sectional dataset of {total_patients} patients (aged 62–85 years) \nwith systemic comorbidities was analyzed. Patients were classified into four \nseverity categories (Normal, Mild, Moderate, Severe) based on DEWS II criteria. \nFive machine learning models — Logistic Regression, Decision Tree, Random Forest, \nSupport Vector Machine (SVM), and K-Nearest Neighbors (KNN) — were trained and \nevaluated using 5-fold stratified cross-validation.\n\nResults: The KNN (k=7) model achieved the best performance with {best_acc}% test \naccuracy and ROC-AUC of {best_auc}. Age showed a moderate correlation with OSDI \n(r={age_osdi_corr}) and TBUT (r={age_tbut_corr}). The 71–75 age group exhibited \nthe highest prevalence of severe dry eye ({sev_counts.get('Severe', 0)} cases, \n{sev_counts.get('Severe', 0)/total_patients*100:.1f}% of cohort). Feature \nimportance analysis revealed AGE as a contributing but not dominant predictor \n(importance={age_imp:.3f}), suggesting that tear film parameters (TBUT, TMH) \nremain the primary clinical indicators.\n\nConclusion: Machine learning models, particularly KNN, can effectively predict \nsevere dry eye risk across age groups. While age contributes to risk \nstratification, clinical tear film parameters remain the strongest predictors. \nAge-specific screening protocols are recommended for patients above 70 years.\n\nKeywords: Dry Eye Disease, Age, Machine Learning, TBUT, OSDI, TMH, KNN\n\n================================================================================\n2. INTRODUCTION\n================================================================================\n\nDry Eye Disease (DED) is one of the most common ocular surface disorders, \ncharacterized by tear film instability, ocular surface inflammation, and \nvisual disturbance. The TFOS DEWS II report estimates global prevalence at \n5–50%, with significant variation by age, gender, and geography.\n\nAge is a well-established risk factor for DED. Physiological changes including \nreduced lacrimal gland secretion, meibomian gland dysfunction, and decreased \ncorneal sensitivity contribute to tear film deterioration in older adults. \nStudies have shown that DED prevalence doubles after the age of 65.\n\nTraditional diagnosis relies on clinical tests:\n  • TBUT (Tear Break-Up Time): Measures tear film stability (<10s = abnormal)\n  • TMH (Tear Meniscus Height): Quantifies tear volume (<0.2mm = deficient)\n  • OSDI (Ocular Surface Disease Index): Patient-reported symptom severity\n  • Blink Rate: Compensatory mechanism for tear film instability\n\nDespite established diagnostic criteria, age-specific risk prediction remains \nunderexplored. Machine learning offers a data-driven approach to integrate \nmultiple clinical parameters and generate personalized risk assessments.\n\nThis study aims to:\n  1. Analyze the relationship between age and dry eye clinical parameters\n  2. Develop ML models to predict severe dry eye from age and clinical data\n  3. Generate age-wise risk stratification for clinical decision support\n\n================================================================================\n3. METHODOLOGY\n================================================================================\n\n3.1 Study Design\n  • Type: Cross-sectional, retrospective analysis\n  • Sample: {total_patients} patients with systemic comorbidities\n  • Age Range: 62–85 years\n  • Gender: {female_pct:.1f}% Female, {100-female_pct:.1f}% Male\n\n3.2 Data Collection\n  • TBUT (Right & Left Eye): Fluorescein dye method, measured in seconds\n  • TMH (Right & Left Eye): Anterior segment OCT, measured in mm\n  • OSDI: 12-item validated questionnaire (0–100 scale)\n  • Blink Rate: Observed blinks per minute\n  • Systemic Disease: Coded comorbidity status\n\n3.3 Target Variable Definition (DEWS II Criteria)\n  • Normal:   OSDI < 13 AND TBUT ≥ 10s\n  • Mild:     OSDI 13–22 OR TBUT 7–9s\n  • Moderate: OSDI 23–33 OR TBUT 4–6s\n  • Severe:   OSDI > 33 OR TBUT ≤ 3s\n\n3.4 Age Grouping\n  • Group 1: 60–65 years (n={len(df[df['AGE_GROUP']=='60-65'])})\n  • Group 2: 66–70 years (n={len(df[df['AGE_GROUP']=='66-70'])})\n  • Group 3: 71–75 years (n={len(df[df['AGE_GROUP']=='71-75'])})\n  • Group 4: 76–85 years (n={len(df[df['AGE_GROUP']=='76-85'])})\n\n3.5 Machine Learning Pipeline\n  • Feature Engineering: AVG_TBUT, AVG_TMH (bilateral average)\n  • Train-Test Split: 80:20 stratified\n  • Feature Scaling: StandardScaler (z-normalization)\n  • Models: Logistic Regression, Decision Tree, Random Forest, SVM, KNN\n  • Validation: 5-Fold Stratified Cross-Validation\n  • Evaluation: Accuracy, Precision, Recall, F1-Score, ROC-AUC\n\n3.6 Statistical Analysis\n  • Pearson & Spearman correlation coefficients\n  • One-way ANOVA across age groups\n  • Chi-Square test of independence\n  • Multivariate Logistic Regression (Odds Ratios)\n  • Significance level: α = 0.05\n\n================================================================================\n4. RESULTS\n================================================================================\n\n4.1 Demographics & Clinical Characteristics\n  • Total patients: {total_patients}\n  • Mean age: {df['AGE'].mean():.1f} ± {df['AGE'].std():.1f} years\n  • Female predominance: {female_pct:.1f}%\n  • Mean OSDI: {df['OSDI'].mean():.1f} ± {df['OSDI'].std():.1f}\n  • Mean TBUT: {df['AVG_TBUT'].mean():.1f} ± {df['AVG_TBUT'].std():.1f} seconds\n  • Mean TMH: {df['AVG_TMH'].mean():.2f} ± {df['AVG_TMH'].std():.2f} mm\n\n4.2 Dry Eye Severity Distribution\n  • Normal:   {sev_counts.get('Normal', 0)} ({sev_counts.get('Normal', 0)/total_patients*100:.1f}%)\n  • Mild:     {sev_counts.get('Mild', 0)} ({sev_counts.get('Mild', 0)/total_patients*100:.1f}%)\n  • Moderate: {sev_counts.get('Moderate', 0)} ({sev_counts.get('Moderate', 0)/total_patients*100:.1f}%)\n  • Severe:   {sev_counts.get('Severe', 0)} ({sev_counts.get('Severe', 0)/total_patients*100:.1f}%)\n\n4.3 Correlation Analysis (Age vs Clinical Parameters)\n  • Age ↔ OSDI:  r = {age_osdi_corr} (p = {age_osdi_p})\n  • Age ↔ TBUT:  r = {age_tbut_corr} (p = {age_tbut_p})\n  • Age ↔ TMH:   r = {table2[table2['Clinical Marker']=='AVG_TMH']['Pearson r'].values[0]}\n  • Age ↔ Blink: r = {table2[table2['Clinical Marker']=='BLINK_RATE']['Pearson r'].values[0]}\n\n4.4 ML Model Performance\n  • Best Model: {best_name}\n  • Test Accuracy: {best_acc}%\n  • ROC-AUC: {best_auc}\n  • 5-Fold CV: {best_cv}% ± {best_cv_std}%\n\n  [See Table 6 for complete model comparison]\n\n4.5 Age-Wise Risk Prediction\n  [See Table 7 for